Tell the application when a window wants typing

An application that types into another client's window with a virtual
keyboard of its own has had to be told by whoever is at the screen that
typing is wanted. A program can say so itself: the text-input protocol is
how it tells the display server that a field of text has the focus, and
the input-method protocol is how the display server passes that on to
whatever supplies the text. With -u Cage now offers the first to the
other clients and the second to the application, and to nobody else: any
other client that would be the input method is told there is no place
for it.

A program says nothing of its fields unless its window has the keyboard
focus: a toolkit does not take a window without it to be one that is
being typed into. So while the application is the input method, the
window it has on show under its own has the keyboard focus for as long
as it is on show, and not only while the application has a virtual
keyboard. What that window is sent is what it was sent before, and no
more: the keys of the application's virtual keyboard. What any other
keyboard does is passed to no client while the focus is lent, as it was.
Having the focus, the window is also the one that is offered the
selection, as any window with the focus is.

The application is told three things. Activate, when a field of text
takes the focus in the window on show; with it, what the field is for,
if the program says - a number, a password - so that the application can
show the keyboard that suits. The same again, without the activate, each
time the program says something more of that field: a toolkit does when
the field is touched again, which is how a keyboard that has been put
away is asked for a second time. And deactivate, when no field has the
focus any more, or the window loses it.

Nothing goes the other way. What a field holds is not passed to the
application, which has no use for it; and what the application might
commit as an input method - a string, a preedit - is not passed to the
program. The application types as before, with the keys of its virtual
keyboard, which are what a program that has no text input at all takes
too. Its own text inputs are told nothing: it is the input method.

This is what lets the OpenGVA HMI's on-screen keyboard come up by itself
when the crew touches a field of a web page, and go away when the field
is left.

Upstream: not submitted

